  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069054",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-127520",
      "knowledge_point_id": "53a1649d-4279-5b7c-839c-728f333b2c3f",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parabola",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069054/assets/60987d053d138d4a26cbc05adccb0da23810f97243f9399d3fe401a0202394b0.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the left-opening parabola shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-8,4).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-8$ and $y=4$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4)^{2} & =4p(-8) \\\\ 16 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
