  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069054",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-127525",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1e118393-2388-5add-b516-e565abe1e36a",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to an Ellipse",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069054/assets/eb0fbc8f57621946a5ec5168e8c607d230269e0c6a64b4c86c835c110f0f6288.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "Since our ellipse is centered at $(0,1)$ and has semiaxes $2$ and $3,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{4}+\\frac{2(y-1)}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(y-1)}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{9x}{4(y-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{3(y-1)}{2x}",
